{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-45064",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=-\\frac{5}{4},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(1)-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =-2-(-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $f(1)=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and $d=-\\frac{3}{4}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+(n-1)(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{3}{4}n+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}n-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\frac{3}{4}n-\\frac{1}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
